Why This Is Asked

Tests practical knowledge of code-splitting, lazy-loading, and real-world budgets, plus how to verify with Lighthouse CI. It also probes how a candidate balances perceived UX with technical constraints in a SaaS dashboard context.

Key Concepts

- Code-splitting at route and panel level
- Visibility gate pattern with IntersectionObserver
- Skeleton UI and native image lazy-loading
- Performance budgets (LCP, TTI) and Lighthouse CI
- CI-based verification and iteration

Code Example

```javascript
// Skeleton VisibilityGate example
import React, { useState, useEffect, useRef } from 'react';

const VisibilityGate = ({children}) => {
  const [show, setShow] = useState(false);
  const ref = useRef(null);
  useEffect(() => {
    const el = ref.current;
    if (!el) return;
    const obs = new IntersectionObserver(([entry]) => {
      if (entry.isIntersecting) { setShow(true); obs.disconnect(); }
    });
    obs.observe(el);
    return () => obs.disconnect();
  }, []);
  return <div ref={ref}>{show ? children : <Skeleton />}</div>;
};
```

Follow-up Questions

- How would you measure impact with Lighthouse budgets and CI?
- How would SSR vs CSR choices affect initial paint for these panels?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design a clean, responsive CSS grid with accessible motion for a news app.

## Key Concepts
- CSS Grid responsive columns (repeat, minmax)
- Image aspect ratio and overlay
- CSS-only entrance animations and stagger
- prefers-reduced-motion and keyboard focus

## Code Example
